      "seed": 935328349,
      "version": 239,
      "versionNonce": 221897427,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1760443574491,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Prompt: \nCreate an object called Blog with 2–5 attributes and 2–5 methods. Do not code,\nwrite in .md format.",
      "fontSize": 5.333333333333344,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Prompt: \nCreate an object called Blog with 2–5 attributes and 2–5 methods. Do not code, write in .md format.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "7bajIE3NJXYuzbIwFk4wT",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": -700,
      "width": 220.00000000000006,
      "height": 153.33333333333363,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#6741d9",
      "backgroundColor": "#fefffe",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b22V",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 472064947,
      "version": 532,
      "versionNonce": 1571088691,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1760446309057,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Comparison\nSimilarities:\nAll three models (my own and the two from the LLM) correctly identified the core of\na Blog object. We all converged on fundamental attributes like title and author for\nidentity, and a posts list to hold the content. Also, all models included methods\noriented for basic actions, approaching CRUD, like adding a post\n(addPost/create_post) and making the content public (publish).\n\nDifferences:\nThe primary difference is in the abstraction. My model is a minimal representation,\nfocusing only on the core components. The LLMs immediately introduced additional,\nattributes that increase complexity and add features.\n\nGrok model added a category, this shows a focus on content organization from the\nstart. \nIt included a deletePost method, building in content management, nearing CRUD\nfunctionality\n\nGPT-4o model designed from the perspective of a single article rather than a\ncollection, it included content and tags directly. \nIts methods (addTag, getSummary) are more singular/indidivudal and focused on a\nsingle piece of content.\n",
      "fontSize": 5.333333333333344,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Comparison\nSimilarities:\nAll three models (my own and the two from the LLM) correctly identified the core of a Blog object. We all converged on fundamental attributes like title and author for identity, and a posts list to hold the content. Also, all models included methods oriented for basic actions, approaching CRUD, like adding a post (addPost/create_post) and making the content public (publish).\n\nDifferences:\nThe primary difference is in the abstraction. My model is a minimal representation, focusing only on the core components. The LLMs immediately introduced additional,  attributes that increase complexity and add features.\n\nGrok model added a category, this shows a focus on content organization from the start. \nIt included a deletePost method, building in content management, nearing CRUD functionality\n\nGPT-4o model designed from the perspective of a single article rather than a collection, it included content and tags directly. \nIts methods (addTag, getSummary) are more singular/indidivudal and focused on a single piece of content.\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Pha9uw9eW2neYJEdOmB95",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1160,
      "y": 0,
      "width": 220.00000000000006,
      "height": 140.00000000000026,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#6741d9",
      "backgroundColor": "#fefffe",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b22d",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1689646035,
      "version": 652,
      "versionNonce": 273908851,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1760445801203,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Comparison\n\nSimilarities\nBoth my system and the AI-generated systems identified the same core entities: a\nconcept for the content container (Blog/Post), a concept for the creator\n(User/Author), and a concept for interaction (Comment). This structure appears to\nbe a common design for this type of problem.\n\nDifferences:\nThe key difference is in the architectural approach.\nMy system is user centered, with the User object as the primary actor who initiates\nall content creation.\n\nThe Grok model is similar but more data/relations oriented. It explicitly links\nobjects back to each other (a Comment knows about its Post, a Post knows its\nUser). This creates an interconnected data graph.\n\nThe GPT-4o model introduces a completely new concept: a BlogManager object.\nInstead of a user model, it proposes a manager model that separates business logic\nfrom the data objects (Blog, Author). This is an advanced design that aims for a\nbigger separation of concepts and concerns.",
      "fontSize": 5.333333333333344,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Comparison\n\nSimilarities\nBoth my system and the AI-generated systems identified the same core entities: a concept for the content container (Blog/Post), a concept for the creator (User/Author), and a concept for interaction (Comment). This structure appears to be a common design for this type of problem.\n\nDifferences:\nThe key difference is in the architectural approach.\nMy system is user centered, with the User object as the primary actor who initiates all content creation.\n\nThe Grok model is similar but more data/relations oriented. It explicitly links objects back to each other (a Comment knows about its Post, a Post knows its User). This creates an interconnected data graph.\n\nThe GPT-4o model introduces a completely new concept: a BlogManager object. Instead of a user model, it proposes a manager model that separates business logic  from the data objects (Blog, Author). This is an advanced design that aims for a bigger separation of concepts and concerns.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "S1LXi2diRH43lU9PKUsQA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 800,
      "y": 500,
      "width": 220.00000000000006,
      "height": 213.33333333333374,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#6741d9",
      "backgroundColor": "#fefffe",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b22h",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1774974114,
      "version": 866,
      "versionNonce": 1655237090,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1760454027617,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "I shared my system diagram with two classmates, Aurelian T. and Victor G., to get feedback\non my design. Their suggestions were very helpful and led to improvements in the logic\nand precision.\n\nSummary \nFrom Aurelian\nHe pointed out that my diagram used the same simple arrow for all connections. He\nexplained that in design diagrams, different arrows show different kinds of relationships.\nHe suggested\nUsing a Composition to show that a Blog owns its Posts.\nUsing a Dependency to show that the User methods use the Post and Comment classes.\n\nFrom Victor\nVictor focused on the logic of the objects\nMy Comment object was missing an author attribute, there was no way to know who wrote\nit.\nThe responsibility for editing a comment was confusing. \nHe suggested that the User object should be the one to perform the edit action, and the\nComment object should just have a simple method to update its own text\n\nChanges Made\n- Duplicated initial version into v2, to clearly showcase progress\n- I updated the arrows to show the correct UML relationships. \n- I used a Composition arrow (a filled diamond) to show that a Blog owns its Posts and a\nPost owns its Comments. \n- I also used a Dependency arrow (a dashed line) to show that the User's methods rely on\nthe Post and Comment classes.\n- I added an author attribute to the Comment class, so every comment is now linked to a\nspecific User.\n- I  improved the logic for editing a comment\nThe Comment class now has a set_text method.\nThe User class now has an edit_comment method",
      "fontSize": 5.333333333333344,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "I shared my system diagram with two classmates, Aurelian T. and Victor G., to get feedback on my design. Their suggestions were very helpful and led to improvements in the logic and precision.\n\nSummary \nFrom Aurelian\nHe pointed out that my diagram used the same simple arrow for all connections. He explained that in design diagrams, different arrows show different kinds of relationships. He suggested\nUsing a Composition to show that a Blog owns its Posts.\nUsing a Dependency to show that the User methods use the Post and Comment classes.\n\nFrom Victor\nVictor focused on the logic of the objects\nMy Comment object was missing an author attribute, there was no way to know who wrote it.\nThe responsibility for editing a comment was confusing. \nHe suggested that the User object should be the one to perform the edit action, and the Comment object should just have a simple method to update its own text\n\nChanges Made\n- Duplicated initial version into v2, to clearly showcase progress\n- I updated the arrows to show the correct UML relationships. \n- I used a Composition arrow (a filled diamond) to show that a Blog owns its Posts and a Post owns its Comments. \n- I also used a Dependency arrow (a dashed line) to show that the User's methods rely on the Post and Comment classes.\n- I added an author attribute to the Comment class, so every comment is now linked to a specific User.\n- I  improved the logic for editing a comment\nThe Comment class now has a set_text method.\nThe User class now has an edit_comment method",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "TVDTYuAsO6h3BIbkKzIdt",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 500,
      "width": 220.00000000000006,
      "height": 100.00000000000018,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#6741d9",
      "backgroundColor": "#fefffe",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b22j",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1355261630,
      "version": 878,
      "versionNonce": 1671172542,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1760454006385,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Conclusion:\n\nThis peer review was helpful and taught me two things:\nFirst, I learned to be precise with design diagrams. The type of arrow  communicates the\nlogical relationship between objects. Using correct symbols for Composition and\nDependency makes system's rules much clearer.\n\nSecond, I learned to think carefully about which object should be responsible for which\naction. \nVictor's feedback helped me understand that the User (the actor) should perform actions,\nwhile objects like Comment should only be responsible for managing their own data. \n\nThis creates a logical and organized system. \nKey point is that design is not about listing objects, but defining the rules and edge cases to\nconnect them.",
      "fontSize": 5.333333333333344,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Conclusion:\n\nThis peer review was helpful and taught me two things:\nFirst, I learned to be precise with design diagrams. The type of arrow  communicates the logical relationship between objects. Using correct symbols for Composition and Dependency makes system's rules much clearer.\n\nSecond, I learned to think carefully about which object should be responsible for which action. \nVictor's feedback helped me understand that the User (the actor) should perform actions, while objects like Comment should only be responsible for managing their own data. \n\nThis creates a logical and organized system. \nKey point is that design is not about listing objects, but defining the rules and edge cases to connect them.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "wksUdHBgLMEEWnAgj60hi",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1280,
      "y": -700,
      "width": 220.00000000000003,
      "height": 86.66666666666683,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#6741d9",
      "backgroundColor": "#fefffe",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b22l",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 33709117,
      "version": 512,
      "versionNonce": 2091733651,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1760445305688,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Conclusion\nWhat I learned from this comparison is how different design philosophies can appear\neven from a simple prompt. My approach was a simple bottom up design, and I\ndesigned the most basic container\nThe LLMs, I suppose they were trained on big amounts of existing code, took a\nmore top-down, feature based approach. \nThey didn't model what a blog is, I see they model what a blog does in a real\napplication. \nThis is surprising, as it shows that LLM abstraction includes the object's identity\nand also its place in a larger system with real user-focused features like\ncategorization and content summarization.\n\n",
      "fontSize": 5.333333333333344,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Conclusion\nWhat I learned from this comparison is how different design philosophies can appear even from a simple prompt. My approach was a simple bottom up design, and I designed the most basic container\nThe LLMs, I suppose they were trained on big amounts of existing code, took a more top-down, feature based approach. \nThey didn't model what a blog is, I see they model what a blog does in a real application. \nThis is surprising, as it shows that LLM abstraction includes the object's identity and also its place in a larger system with real user-focused features like categorization and content summarization.\n\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "ZwbmQ0Mnvyy1nIM-zRN41",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1160,
      "y": 160,
      "width": 220.00000000000003,
      "height": 93.33333333333351,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#6741d9",
      "backgroundColor": "#fefffe",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b22t",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1828040957,
      "version": 761,
      "versionNonce": 1820419101,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1760445964021,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Conclusion\nThis test of LLMs shows the difference between a simple, direct model and complex,\nscalable architecture. \nMy system is straightforward and easy to understand. \nThe LLM systems, I see that GPT-4o, introduced a more complex and scale focused\ndesign by using a BlogManager. \nIn hindsight, this shows an OOP principle, separating data containers from the logic\nthat manages them. \n\nThe introduction of a manager wasn't something I considered, and I consider it has\na more scalable approach for a system that could grow in complexity and users.\n\nThis is a lesson in thinking about not just the objects, but the overall\narchitecture/system that will orchestrate them.",
      "fontSize": 5.333333333333344,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Conclusion\nThis test of LLMs shows the difference between a simple, direct model and complex, scalable architecture. \nMy system is straightforward and easy to understand. \nThe LLM systems, I see that GPT-4o, introduced a more complex and scale focused design by using a BlogManager. \nIn hindsight, this shows an OOP principle, separating data containers from the logic that manages them. \n\nThe introduction of a manager wasn't something I considered, and I consider it has a more scalable approach for a system that could grow in complexity and users.\n\nThis is a lesson in thinking about not just the objects, but the overall architecture/system that will orchestrate them.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
